      'MAIN CONTENT\n\n1. The BCEAO regulatory framework — UEMOA zone\n\nThe BCEAO governs SFDs via Instruction No. 005-06-2010 on the constitutive elements of the SFD licensing application file and Instruction No. 007-06-2010 on control and sanction procedures for SFDs.\n\nKey requirements:\n- Minimum liquidity ratio: 100% (liquid assets / short-term resources)\n- ALM Committee: mandatory for category 2 and 3 SFDs, minimum quarterly meetings\n- Prudential reporting: monthly transmission of statements to the BCEAO\n\nSource: BCEAO, Instruction No. 010-08-2010 on SFD prudential rules — bceao.int\n\n2. How liquidity risk destroys a profitable SFD\n\nMechanism:\n- Day 1: An SFD has 500M FCFA in demand deposits and 400M FCFA in 18-month loans\n- Day 30: 15% of depositors withdraw funds (75M FCFA)\n- Day 45: The SFD cannot honor withdrawals. It is technically insolvent.\n- Result: Provisional administration by the UMOA Banking Commission\n\nThe paradox: The SFD was profitable (positive ROE). It died from lack of liquidity, not lack of profitability.\n\n3. The 3 indicators your CFO must monitor weekly\n\nIndicator 1: 0-90 day liquidity gap\n- Calculation: Liquid assets (0-90 days) — Callable liabilities (0-90 days)\n- Critical threshold: positive. A negative gap exposes the SFD to a withdrawal shock.\n\nIndicator 2: BCEAO liquidity ratio\n- Calculation: Liquid assets / Short-term resources\n- Threshold: 100% minimum. Below: alert zone for the UMOA Banking Commission.\n\nIndicator 3: Liquidity cushion\n- Calculation: (Liquid assets — Immediate callable liabilities) / Total deposits\n- Recommended threshold: 10% minimum. Below 5%: exposure to mass withdrawal.\n\n4. Stress tests: 3 critical scenarios\n\nScenario 1 — Mass withdrawal: 20% of depositors withdraw funds within 30 days.\nScenario 2 — Non-renewal of refinancing line: BOAD or BCEAO does not renew a 200M FCFA line.\nScenario 3 — Seasonal crisis: Agricultural campaign (October-December) with concurrent 30% withdrawals from rural depositors.',

