      'MAIN CONTENT\n\n1. The COBAC regulatory framework — CEMAC zone\n\nCOBAC governs EMFs via the EMF-2017 Regulation.\n\nEMF classification:\n- Category 1: deposit collection from members and lending\n- Category 2: public deposit collection and lending to third parties\n- Category 3: lending without deposit collection\n\nKey requirements:\n- Minimum liquidity ratio: 100%\n- ALM Committee: mandatory for category 2 and 3 EMFs\n- Key difference from UEMOA: COBAC supervises both commercial banks AND EMFs\n\nSource: COBAC, EMF-2017 Regulation — beac.int (COBAC is an organ of the BEAC)\n\n2. Specificities of liquidity risk in the CEMAC zone\n\nDependence on external resources: Many CEMAC EMFs depend on refinancing lines from international donors (BOAD, BEAC, development banks). Non-renewal of a line creates an immediate gap.\n\nSeasonal withdrawals: In CEMAC, mass withdrawals occur during cocoa and coffee campaigns (Cameroon, October-March). These movements are predictable but rarely provisioned.\n\nUnderdeveloped interbank market: The CEMAC interbank market is less liquid than in UEMOA. An EMF under pressure has fewer emergency refinancing options.\n\n3. The 3 critical indicators in the CEMAC zone\n\nIndicator 1: COBAC liquidity ratio\n- Threshold: 100% minimum. Below: possible COBAC intervention.\n\nIndicator 2: Dependence on external refinancing\n- Calculation: External resources / Total resources\n- Critical threshold: beyond 40%, the EMF is vulnerable to non-renewal.\n\nIndicator 3: Deposit concentration\n- Calculation: Deposits of top 10 clients / Total deposits\n- Critical threshold: beyond 25%, withdrawal of one large client creates a crisis.',

